1
00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.604
Let's build our first identity pipeline.

2
00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:08.641
In this lesson, you will be building
a very simple self-reflecting agent

3
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.778
by building a Hastag pipeline
that is able to loop

4
00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:15.582
this looping mechanism will allow you
to implement the self reflection.

5
00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:18.885
As a very simple example,
you will have an LM generate

6
00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:22.822
named entities from unstructured texts
and iteratively improve

7
00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.724
on its response with self-reflection.

8
00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.126
Let's dive into the code.

9
00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:30.296
Self-reflection is a mechanism
for self-assessment and correction,

10
00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.232
and it allows us to refine outputs

11
00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:35.702
by providing more reliable
and accurate information.

12
00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:36.603
With self-reflection,

13
00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:40.907
we allow the NLM to provide
feedback to itself for future trials.

14
00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.109
We can have the model provide the self

15
00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:46.980
with a success status,
or allow an external source to do so,

16
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.450
but then we have it self
formulate specific feedback.

17
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.119
We can go even further
and get the NLM to reflect on

18
00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:56.689
what actions
it can take to improve on the response,

19
00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:00.160
and even provided with some criteria
to look out for.

20
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.563
It can then verbally states
what went wrong and how to improve.

21
00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.499
It's in the next iterations.

22
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:06.866
In this

23
00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.970
lab, we're going to be building
a very simple self-reflecting agent

24
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.508
where you'll ask and then allow them to
generate entities from unstructured text.

25
00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:19.312
At each iteration,
the NLM will either state that it is done

26
00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:23.383
so it's reflected on the output,
and it is happy with the results,

27
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.085
or it will reflect and critique

28
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:28.855
on what should be improved
about the extracted entities.

29
00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:33.359
We're going to be building this thanks to
the availability of custom components.

30
00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:37.297
And we're going to create a component
that we call the entities validator

31
00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:42.297
that will simply evaluate whether the
generator decided that it was done or not.

32
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.705
And until the generator decides
that it's done,

33
00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.641
it will continue looping.

34
00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.543
Another way these types of looping

35
00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:53.980
pipelines can be super
useful is to validate outputs.

36
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.217
For example,
we extract data in Json format,

37
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.220
but we want it to adhere
to a certain schema.

38
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.223
In some cases,
we could implement output validators

39
00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:06.926
that return both the invalid replies
and the error messages

40
00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:10.730
we got from a validator
such as pedantic, where we can validate

41
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:14.134
whether an output adheres to our Json
schema.

42
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:18.371
Let's see how we can create the
entities generator in code.

43
00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.840
Let's
start with the usual warning suppression,

44
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.777
and make sure we load
all of our environment variables.

45
00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.746
Next let's import all of the dependencies

46
00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:29.215
for everything
we'll be using in this lesson.

47
00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:33.753
As a first step, we'll start by creating
a very simple custom haystack components

48
00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.956
that we're going to be calling
the entities validator.

49
00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.658
Let's see what this component does.

50
00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.661
Let's start by creating our class.

51
00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.230
We call it the entities validator.

52
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.701
And let's have it have a run method
that is expecting replies.

53
00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:53.306
These replies are going to be coming
from an alarm.

54
00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:56.309
What we want to do here is very,
very simple.

55
00:02:56.409 --> 00:03:00.813
We're going to have this component output
one of two outputs.

56
00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:05.813
If the replies contains the word done,
we're going to be outputting entities.

57
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.154
If however it does

58
00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:11.958
not include dumb
we're going to be doing two things.

59
00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:16.796
This means the NLM has decided
that it's not done creating entities yet.

60
00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.766
So we'll have this print out

61
00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.702
the entities that the NLM
is still reflecting on and read.

62
00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:26.539
This will allow us to see the
and visualize the loops easily,

63
00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:31.477
but it will also output the entities
in and output called entities to validate.

64
00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.981
The last thing we have to do
is make sure that we're providing these

65
00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:38.885
as output types
with a decorator to the run method.

66
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:40.086
In this case, you'll notice

67
00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:44.123
that this has two outputs
entities to validate and entities.

68
00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.892
Both of them a string.

69
00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:49.562
Let's see how we might use a component
like this.

70
00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:50.763
For example.

71
00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:54.968
Let's create an entities
validator and again

72
00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:59.172
let's replicate a reply that we might get
from another level.

73
00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:02.775
For example,
let's run this entity validator

74
00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:07.614
with a response from that column
that has the entity name Tawana.

75
00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:11.251
As you can see, there is no such thing
as done in this response.

76
00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:14.887
So hopefully we'll be printing out
these entities

77
00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:19.192
in red and returning
them as entities to validate.

78
00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.426
Great.

79
00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:24.364
What if we had a response
that included the word done.

80
00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:25.865
Let's give that a go.

81
00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:29.102
In this case,
the entities validator is outputting

82
00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:32.538
the entities in entities.

83
00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:36.309
Now let's start to create a special type
of prompt template.

84
00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:39.279
In this prompt template
we're going to have an if block.

85
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.680
You'll see why.

86
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.214
Let's start

87
00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:45.985
by creating the first instruction
we want to give to the language model.

88
00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:50.657
This template includes the instruction
extract entities from the following text.

89
00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:55.395
And as you can see, our prompt builder is
going to be expected an input called text

90
00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.597
while also providing it
with some information

91
00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:01.567
of what kind of entities
we want to extract from this text.

92
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.437
The entities should be presented
in key value pairs.

93
00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:09.275
We're telling it to extract person,
location and date while also telling it

94
00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:12.278
if there are no possibilities
for a particular category.

95
00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:15.648
Return an empty list and so on.

96
00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:20.648
Now this is great, but it will only allow
the end to produce a response once.

97
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.957
And it's not giving us a chance
to iterate on this response.

98
00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:27.493
So we're going to be using the if else

99
00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.563
block functionality
that comes with Jinja templating.

100
00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:32.699
So let's start.

101
00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:33.900
Let's make this be

102
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.338
in the else block of our
if else statement.

103
00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:40.973
And you'll see what we add in the
if statement.

104
00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:44.143
Now in the
if statement, we want to give instructions

105
00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:48.047
to the large language model
if it has entities to validate.

106
00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:51.584
So we're going to be providing it
with some examples

107
00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.754
of what it should look out
for to improve on its response.

108
00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:57.890
in this case
our prompt builder is going to be checking

109
00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.160
if it has entities to validate as inputs.

110
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.629
And then we're going to be adding
some more information

111
00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:06.666
that it can use to improve on its answer.

112
00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:07.667
We again iterate.

113
00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:08.668
Here was a text.

114
00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:11.471
You were provided and provided the text.

115
00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:14.907
And here are the entities
you previously extracted.

116
00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.844
And then we give it some bullet points
on what it can reflect on

117
00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:19.746
and what it can improve.

118
00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:23.583
This is where we also tell the length
that it can say I'm done

119
00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:24.817
if it thinks it's done.

120
00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:27.820
This is a very,
very simple way of implementing

121
00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.823
self reflection
in these types of pipelines.

122
00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:33.126
Let's see how this one works.

123
00:06:33.126 --> 00:06:37.330
Now that we have our prompt template
that is able to accept entities

124
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.001
to validate if they exist, we can start
creating our self-reflecting agents.

125
00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:44.771
Let's start
by creating our prompt builder.

126
00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:48.641
This prompt builder is going to be
using the templates we just created.

127
00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:51.177
Next we want to have our generator.

128
00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:55.314
In this case again I'll be using
the OpenAI generator with a default model.

129
00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:57.850
So it's going to be GPT three.

130
00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:02.221
And finally we want to be able
to use our entities validator somehow.

131
00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.525
Another important thing to note here
is that because this pipeline

132
00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:07.059
is going to be looping,

133
00:07:07.059 --> 00:07:12.059
we'll also able to provide the maximum
loops while allowing our pipeline to have.

134
00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:15.168
This allows you to make sure
that you're not doing more requests

135
00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:18.704
than you want to to a model provider
like OpenAI.

136
00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:22.775
In this case, we're telling it
it can do a maximum of ten loops.

137
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:25.812
If the line isn't able to create an asset,

138
00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:29.215
satisfied with within ten loops,
our pipeline will fail.

139
00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:32.785
But it also means that we're not incurring
more cost than we want to.

140
00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:35.855
Next thing we'll do
is we'll simply add all these components

141
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:39.025
to our pipeline,
but let's see how we connect them.

142
00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:43.629
So we simply start
by connecting our prompts to our alarm.

143
00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.298
This time we're being a bit specific.

144
00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:50.069
You don't need to provide prompt
in each case, but if you want you can.

145
00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:53.639
And then we're going to be providing
the replies from this large language

146
00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:56.042
model to our entities. Validator.

147
00:07:56.042 --> 00:08:00.413
Now the entities validator has a response
from the large language model

148
00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:01.714
that it can evaluate

149
00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:05.084
and see whether the large language
model thinks it's done or not.

150
00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.987
However,
if it does have entities to validate,

151
00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:11.691
we want these
to be looping back into our prompt.

152
00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:16.691
Notice how here, if there are entities
to validate as outputs from our entities

153
00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:21.601
validator, they're going to be added
to prompt builder entities to validate.

154
00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:23.035
Remember how the first

155
00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:27.173
if statement in our prompt had
if there are entities to validate.

156
00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:29.141
This is where that loop happens.

157
00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:31.210
Now this is our first looping pipeline.

158
00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:35.214
So again let's use the show utility
to see what's happening here.

159
00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:38.751
Let's say Self-reflecting agent Dot, show.

160
00:08:38.751 --> 00:08:41.320
And as you can see, we're noticing a loop.

161
00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:45.791
In this case, our prompt builder
is providing a full prompt to our large

162
00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:50.530
language model, which is then forwarding
its replies to the entities validator.

163
00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:55.101
And the entities validator
is either responding with entities,

164
00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:58.471
or it's looping back
with entities to validate.

165
00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:03.009
And this loop continues
until either we've hit ten loops all.

166
00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:06.078
The response has the word done in it.

167
00:09:06.078 --> 00:09:08.714
Now that we have our pipeline,
this provides some text

168
00:09:08.714 --> 00:09:13.352
to our prompt builder and see if
then can generate the named entities.

169
00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:15.488
Here I've provided a dummy text.

170
00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:19.559
It's about Istanbul,
the population of Istanbul, and so on.

171
00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:23.763
Let's run our Self-reflecting agent
by providing the prompt builder

172
00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.131
with this text.

173
00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:28.134
We know that our entities
validator will print out

174
00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:31.537
any entities
that are still to be validated and read.

175
00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:35.207
As an extra step,
we can also print out the final response.

176
00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:37.743
Once the looping is done in green.

177
00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:40.846
So notice here
that every time you see red,

178
00:09:40.846 --> 00:09:43.482
it's still reflecting on those entities.

179
00:09:43.482 --> 00:09:46.319
The final result will be printed out
in green.

180
00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:47.553
All right.

181
00:09:47.553 --> 00:09:52.224
As you can see here, the first time
the NLM created the entities

182
00:09:52.224 --> 00:09:55.394
it had location
but it didn't have personal data.

183
00:09:55.428 --> 00:09:58.497
Even though we asked for person
and date to be included,

184
00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:03.469
even if there are no entities to extract,
we asked for there to be an empty list.

185
00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:07.139
However, in the second iteration,
when it was done,

186
00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:11.611
it did in fact create person and data
as entities.

187
00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.679
Let's try another example.

188
00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:17.183
In this case
I provided another dummy bit of text,

189
00:10:17.183 --> 00:10:20.820
but this time it's a transcript
